    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d154">

                <pb facs="0244" n="160" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp02/pg160"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72111Y2/11</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Germany</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Gerard</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1917-02-04T16:00:00+01:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">February 4, 1917, 4 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received February 5, 3.25
                        p.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>4993. In conversation with Zimmermann<anchor n="2" xml:id="d154-f1"/> regarding the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Yarrow-dale</hi> he referred to the treaty of 1798 [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">1799</hi>] about subjects of both countries having nine
                    months to leave in case of war and take their goods with them. He said he and I
                    ought to draw up a sort of protocol about this. Please instruct. I think we have
                    many Germans. There are very few real Americans left in Germany.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gerard</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d154-f1" n="2" xml:id="d154-f1-content">
                <p>Dr. Artur Zimmermann, German Secretary of
                    State for Foreign Affairs.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
